course: biology
lesson: 290
title: Insects as food in other countries
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At a night market in Thailand a child your age buys a paper cone of fried crickets the way you might buy chips. They taste nutty and crunchy, and for her they are just a normal snack.
explain: About two billion people around the world eat insects, and more than 1,900 kinds are eaten: grasshoppers in Mexico, big caterpillars in southern Africa, queen ants in Colombia, locusts in parts of the Middle East and Africa.
explain: Insects are packed with protein and need far less land, water and food than cows, so some scientists think they could help feed the world.
explain: The yuck feeling many Europeans have is learned, not built in: in America lobster was once cheap food for the poor, and now it is a luxury. But wild bugs from the garden are never a snack, because they can carry germs and poisons.
fact: About 2 billion people eat insects, and more than 1,900 kinds of insect are eaten around the world.
fact: Crickets and mealworms have about as much protein as meat by weight, plus good fats and minerals.
fact: Farming insects needs much less land, water and food than farming cows, and makes far less greenhouse gas, the kind of gas that warms the planet.
fact: Chapulines are toasted grasshoppers with lime and chilli, eaten in Oaxaca in Mexico for thousands of years and sold at markets and football games.
fact: Mopane worms are big caterpillars eaten in southern Africa; they are dried, stored for months and are an important food and income for families.
fact: In Colombia big-bottomed queen ants are toasted and eaten like peanuts, and in Australia Aboriginal people eat honeypot ants, which store sweet liquid in their bodies.
fact: The red food colour carmine is made from crushed cochineal bugs that live on cactus, and it is used in sweets, yoghurt and lipstick; the Aztecs used it to dye cloth.
fact: Flour and fruit can contain tiny insect parts, food rules allow a small harmless amount, and it is impossible to grow food with no insects at all.
fact: Wild insects can carry germs, poisons or garden sprays, and some are toxic, so insects for food are farmed and cooked safely, and people allergic to shellfish may react to insects.
fact: Black soldier fly larvae eat food waste and are turned into food for chickens and farmed fish, which saves wild fish from being caught as feed.
fact: In the Bible John the Baptist ate locusts and wild honey, and locusts are still eaten in parts of the Middle East and Africa.
fact: A big locust swarm can eat as much food in a day as millions of people.
fact: Disgust at food is learned from family and culture, and tastes change over time: lobster was once food for the poor and prisoners in America.
exercise: How many people around the world eat insects? | About 2 billion | Only about 2 hundred | Nobody, it is a myth | Everyone on Earth
exercise: What do fried crickets taste like, people say? | Nutty and crunchy | Sweet and sticky like jam | Like fizzy lemonade | Of nothing at all, like air
exercise: Why are crickets called a good food? | They are full of protein, good fats and minerals | They are pure sugar | They have no goodness at all, only crunch | They are made mostly of water and air inside
exercise: Why could insect farms be kinder to the planet than cattle farms? | They need far less land, water and food and make less greenhouse gas | Insects breathe out fresh oxygen, just like the plants and trees do | Insect farms need no buildings or care | Insects eat plastic waste
exercise: What are chapulines? | Toasted grasshoppers with lime and chilli from Mexico | Fried caterpillars from Africa | Ant eggs in Thai soup | Crickets dipped in dark chocolate, a sweet from France
exercise: Where are mopane worms eaten? | In southern Africa | In the far north of Norway | In the mountains of Japan | In the north of Canada
exercise: How do families keep mopane worms for months? | They dry them | They keep them alive in jars | They freeze them in snow | They soak them in milk
exercise: Which insects are eaten like peanuts in Colombia? | Big-bottomed queen ants | Butterflies with their wings off | Ladybirds, dried in the sun | Honey bees and their wax
exercise: Where does the red colour carmine come from? | Crushed cochineal bugs from cactus | Red beetroot juice boiled down | Tomato skins | Strawberry seeds, crushed and dried
exercise: Are tiny insect bits in flour dangerous? | No, a small amount is harmless and allowed | Yes, they are always poisonous | Yes, they make bread go blue | There are never any insects at all in flour, so the question is silly
exercise: Why should you never eat a bug from the garden? | It may carry germs, poisons or sprays | It is too small to fill you up | Garden bugs taste of nothing at all | It would hatch and grow inside your tummy
exercise: Who might react badly to eating insects? | People allergic to shellfish | People who like chips | People with brown eyes | People who are very tall
exercise: What do black soldier fly larvae do on farms? | Eat food waste and become food for chickens and fish | Pollinate the apple trees in spring | Make honey and wax for the farmer | Guard the hens from foxes at night by buzzing
exercise: Who in the Bible ate locusts and wild honey? | John the Baptist | Noah | King David | Moses's sister Miriam
exercise: Where does the feeling that insects are disgusting come from? | It is learned from family and culture | Everyone is born with it, all over the world | It comes from eating far too many insects | Only children ever feel it, never adults
exercise: What food was once cheap food for the poor in America and is now a luxury? | Lobster | Bread | Potatoes | Apples
test: «ew, who eats bugs, nobody normal» What is right? | About 2 billion people eat insects as a normal food | Nobody in the world eats them | Only people in films and TV shows eat bugs, for a dare | Only animals eat bugs
test: «my strawberry yoghurt is pink cos of strawberries, end of» What might also be true? | Its red colour may come from carmine, made from cochineal bugs | Pink yoghurt is coloured with paint | The colour comes from the milk of a special breed of pink cow | It is pink because it is cold
test: «found a fat beetle in the garden, dare you to eat it» What is the right answer? | No, wild bugs can carry germs, poisons or sprays | Yes, all garden beetles are tasty and good for you | Yes, if you wash it with water | Only if it is still alive
test: Count them: a Thai snack cone has 10 crickets. How many legs? | 60 | 40 | 80 | 10
test: Count them: 4 mopane caterpillars, each has 6 true legs. How many true legs? | 24 | 16 | 32 | 40
test: What would happen if a farmer swapped fish caught at sea for soldier fly larvae to feed his farmed fish? | Fewer wild fish would be caught for feed | The farmed fish would starve | The sea would get emptier of wild fish faster | The larvae would eat the fish
test: «lobster's always been posh food» What is right? | In America it was once cheap food for the poor and prisoners | Yes, only kings and queens ever ate it, from the very start | Lobster was never eaten before cars were invented | Lobster is a kind of insect
test: In a cartoon a lizard catches a fly and burps happily. A boy says only lizards eat bugs. What is right? | Many people eat insects too, in many countries | Only reptiles can digest insects, because of their special stomachs | Insects are poisonous to all mammals | Only birds and lizards eat insects
test: «a locust swarm, so what, it's a few grasshoppers» What is the danger? | A big swarm can eat as much food in a day as millions of people | Locusts only eat wood | Swarms are always tiny | Locusts eat only other insects, so a swarm keeps the crops safe from pests
test: «we're getting insect burgers at school, can my mate with a prawn allergy have one» What is the careful answer? | He should check first, as shellfish allergy can mean a reaction to insects | Yes, insects can never cause allergies, so he can eat as many burgers as he likes | No, insect burgers are poisonous to everyone | Yes, if he drinks milk with it
test: Why would mopane worms be a good food to keep for a dry season? | Dried, they last for months | They grow back again every single night | They must be eaten fresh the same day | They turn into butterflies in the jar
exam: Identify the principal nutritional merit of edible insects such as crickets and mealworms. | A high content of protein, together with fats and minerals | A high content of sugar and nothing else of nutritional value | An absence of any nutritional value | A high content of water exclusively
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colouring agent carmine. | It is obtained from crushed cochineal insects and employed in confectionery and cosmetics | It is extracted from tomato skins | It is a synthetic colour unrelated to any animal, manufactured from coal tar in laboratories | It is derived from red clay
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the consumption of wild insects from a garden is inadvisable. | They may carry pathogens, toxins or residues of pesticides | They are invariably larger than farmed insects | They possess no protein whatsoever | They are protected by law in every country
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the aversion to insects as food prevalent in parts of Europe? | It is learned from family and culture and may alter over time | It is an innate and universal response of all humans | It arises from the poisonous nature of every insect | It is confined to children
